From f73e94f1e7d068fa7edaa953e6e46e9f97d93eae Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: shaurizard <contact@nyoom.engineering>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 12:09:18 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08/32] memory: give guest physical memory a section committed
 when it is created

Guest virtual and physical memory shared one page file-backed section
created with SEC_RESERVE, and Memory::Initialize committed the 512 MB
physical range afterwards (AllocFixed over the physical heap, from
xenia-canary 5f5be0668). The heaps then committed physical pages again on
every allocation, and the GPU's write watches change the protection of
physical pages thousands of times a second. Under Wine, a protection
change on a view of a section that commits on demand goes through
wineserver, 5-7 times the cost of the same call on a committed section.

Map guest physical memory from a section of its own, created with
SEC_COMMIT; guest virtual memory keeps the reserve-only section. The
map_info entries whose target lies above 4 GB (the physical views) map
from the new section at target - 4 GB (ViewSource), on Windows, Linux
and macOS. Heaps of guest physical memory set the protection where they
used to commit (BaseHeap::AllocHostPages), and AddVirtualMappedRange
protects instead of committing the MMIO windows at 0x7F000000, which
sit in physical memory. The heaps never decommit host pages
(BaseHeap::Decommit only updates the page table), so the physical
section stays committed for its lifetime.

Part of the runtime changes that took Project Gotham Racing 4 on a Steam
Deck under Proton from 78.7 to 89.4 fps (pgr4-native README, "Steam
Deck (OLED)": "a page protection change no longer costs a commit
through wineserver"); no run measures it on its own.

Co-authored-by: factory-droid[bot] <138933559+factory-droid[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>
